# What not to commit.
#
# Everything the course hands you is already tracked, so nothing here can hide
# an assignment or a dataset from you.  These rules only keep *new* clutter out
# of your repository -- the files your tools generate while you work, which are
# not the work itself.

## Jupyter checkpoints.
#
# Jupyter saves a checkpoint copy beside every notebook you open.  It is a
# stale duplicate of your own work: committing it means carrying two versions
# of the same assignment, and the old one starts conflicting with `git pull`
# every time an assignment is revised.  JupyterLab's `.virtual_documents` is
# the same idea for its editor.
.ipynb_checkpoints/
.virtual_documents/

## Python's build noise.
#
# Python caches a compiled copy of every module it imports.  These are
# machine-specific, regenerated on demand, and of no interest to anyone.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/

## Virtual environments.
#
# `uv sync` rebuilds these from uv.lock, which *is* tracked.  The environment
# itself is large, machine-specific, and would be wrong on anyone else's
# computer anyway.
.venv/
venv/

## Datasets and media.
#
# Large binaries do not belong in a repository that is pulled every week.  Any
# data the course ships you is already tracked, so these rules only stop you
# committing something new by accident -- a figure you exported, a file you
# downloaded.  Your code is what is marked, and .gitattributes already strips
# notebook outputs, so a plot you made lives in your notebook, not as a file.
#
# If an assignment ever does ask you to commit one of these, `git add -f` says
# you meant it.
*.h5
*.hdf5
*.npz
*.png
*.jpg
*.jpeg
*.gif
*.wav
*.mp3
*.mp4

## macOS leaves these everywhere.
.DS_Store
